## Previously raised findings (review-round registry)

These defects were raised on earlier review rounds of this PR. For each finding below: verify fixed (cite the fixing hunk), withdraw (reason required), or mark re-introduced. New claims need new evidence — a claim already on this list is answered, not re-raised.

| id | status | severity | file | claim |
|----|--------|----------|------|-------|
| F1 | open | IMPORTANT | test/ci_2408_rosetta_interpreter_guard_test.dart | ⚠️ important the ac2 guard never runs — the bare dart exemption is shipped unpinned rosettacommand test ci 2408 rosetta interpreter guard test dart 39 anchors with and no multiline true but in the ac2 group it is applied to whole multi line run scripts not per line with hasmatch matches only at string start so scripts line 167 171 keeps a step only if its run block literally begins with arch x86 64 — no script in this repo does all three start with comments or variable assignments every job therefore fails usesrosettadart and hits continue at line 182 evidence run against this tree dart test test ci 2408 rosetta interpreter guard test dart → 4 tests all ac1 4 all tests passed the ac2 — the bare dart exemption stays paired with setup dart arch group registers zero tests — for the 3 jobs that actually run arch x86 64 dart build macos yml build macos cli ci yml binaries ci yml binaries pr the same filter chain with per line matching detects those 3 jobs the shipped code detects 0 consequence the file header documents ac2 as part of the guard s contract every job invoking arch x86 64 dart carries a setup dart step with an explicit architecture and the pr description claims the exemption is itself pinned per job — but removing architecture matrix arch from any of the three setup dart steps today leaves this guard green and ac1 s bare dart exemption would then path resolve the host arm64 dart into arch x86 64 dart build cli — the exact bad cpu type in executable failure class this pr exists to prevent unguarded ac1 and the fa clip regression pin do work this is only the dart exemption half that is inert fix ac2 doesn t need its own matcher — derive the job set from the already correct per line helper dart final dartjobs rosetainvocations path where inv inv 3 dart map inv inv 1 toset for final job in dartjobs existing setup dart architecture assert lines 167 182 are then deleted wholesale minimal alternative regexp multiline true plus a per line filter before hasmatch — but note the inner allmatches on the comment filtered re joined script has the same start of string anchor problem so reusing rosettainvocations is the safer fix |
| F2 | open | SUGGESTION | test/ci_2408_rosetta_interpreter_guard_test.dart | 💡 suggestion the guard s matcher only sees line leading arch x86 64 forms regexp r s arch x86 64 s matches only invocations at the start of a line shell legal forms that path resolve the same way are invisible to the guard e g yaml cd scripts arch x86 64 bash build fa clip sh out arch x86 64 bash scripts build fa clip sh sudo arch x86 64 bash scripts build fa clip sh any future edit that moves the rosetta invocation into a compound command sails through ac1 while reintroducing the gh 2408 failure class consider dropping the anchor comments are already skipped before matching and a stray false positive in a guard test is cheap or anchoring on shell operators regexp r s arch x86 64 s optional also scan github actions action y a ml composite steps so arch x86 64 can t hide outside workflows — the repo already ships a composite sqlite3 native assets prewarm |

### How to answer

Add a `findingReview` block to `outputs/pr_review.json` (alongside `inlineComments`):

```json
{"findingReview": {
  "dispositions": [
    {"n": 1, "status": "fixed", "hunk": "<file:lines \u2014 the fixing hunk>"},
    {"n": 2, "status": "withdrawn", "reason": "<why this is not a defect>"},
    {"n": 3, "status": "re-introduced"}
  ],
  "confirmations": [
    {"proposedFor": 1, "same": true}
  ]
}}
```

Unconfirmed candidates become new identities (the merge bias is confirm-only); a genuinely new finding on a resolved item is expected to be raised again with new evidence.

